      </li>
      <li>These terms are governed by Indian law, and disputes go to the courts at Kozhikode, Kerala.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This summary is here to help. The full terms below are what apply.</p>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="about">1. About these terms</h2>
  <p>
    These Terms of Service ("Terms") are an agreement between you and Navaneeth CT, an individual based in India,
    trading as Emploist ("we", "us", or "our"). "You", "your", and "Customer" mean the business or organization that
    uses Emploist.
  </p>

  <h3>Who can use Emploist</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Emploist is for businesses and other organizations that are hiring. It isn't for personal, family, or household
      use, and we don't offer it to consumers.
    </li>
    <li>
      The person who creates your company in Emploist or starts its Plan must be at least 18 years old and have the
      authority to accept these Terms for the organization. By doing so, that person confirms that both are true.
    </li>
    <li>Everyone on your team who uses Emploist must be at least 18 years old.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>How you accept these Terms</h3>
  <p>
    You accept these Terms when you sign in to Emploist, create a company, start a Plan, or otherwise use Emploist. The
    sign-in and invitation pages tell you that by continuing, you agree to our Terms of Service. If you don't agree,
    don't use Emploist.
  </p>
  <p>
    Team members use Emploist on their company's behalf. Each team member must follow these Terms, and the company is
    responsible for their use.
  </p>
  <p>
    People who apply for jobs through a careers page don't need an account and aren't bound by these Terms. Our Privacy
    Policy explains <a href="/privacy/#applicants">how applicants' data is handled</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>What our agreement includes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      these Terms, including the data processing terms in <a href="#data-processing">section 13</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      our <a href="/privacy/#subprocessors">list of subprocessors</a>, which is part of the data processing terms and is
      published in our Privacy Policy
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Our <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> explains how we handle personal data as a controller, such as your
    team's account details. Apart from the list of subprocessors, it isn't part of our agreement.
  </p>
  <p>
    If anything conflicts, the data processing terms win over the rest of these Terms and over the Privacy Policy for
    anything about personal data in Customer Data. Where the Standard Contractual Clauses apply (see section 13), they
    win over both. Your purchase from Dodo Payments is also covered by Dodo's own Buyer Terms (see section 5).
  </p>

  <h3>Words we use</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>"Emploist"</strong> or <strong>"the service"</strong> means the Emploist web app, the careers pages and emails
      it provides, and our related support.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Your company"</strong> means the company you set up in Emploist. It holds your jobs, careers page, team,
      settings, and applications.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Team members"</strong> means the people who belong to your company in Emploist, including admins and members.
    </li>
    <li><strong>"Applicants"</strong> means people who apply to your jobs through Emploist.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Customer Data"</strong> means the content you and your team members put into Emploist, such as jobs, careers
      page content and code, application forms, emails, and comments, and the information applicants submit through it,
      such as their names, contact details, resumes, and answers, together with the match scores and other records
      Emploist creates from them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Plan"</strong> means your company's Emploist subscription, including its free trial. Your Plan is <strong
        >"active"</strong
      > while it's in its free trial, paid up, or in the grace period after a failed payment.
    </li>
    <li><strong>"Credits"</strong> means the AI credits used to rank applications.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="accounts">2. Accounts, companies, and roles</h2>

  <h3>Signing in</h3>
  <p>
    You sign in with a Google or Microsoft account, or with a one-time code we email to you. Emploist doesn't use
    passwords, so anyone who controls your email or your Google or Microsoft account can sign in as you. Keep them
    secure, don't share sign-in codes or invitation links, and don't let anyone else use your account.
  </p>

  <h3>Your company</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Each person's account can belong to one company at a time.</li>
    <li>Give accurate information about your company, including its name, and keep it up to date.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Admins and members</h3>
  <p>Your company has two roles:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Admins</strong> manage billing (starting and changing the Plan, buying credit packs, managing payment details and invoices,
      and canceling), company settings, the careers page and its address and code, the company-wide switches for AI
      ranking and for emails to applicants, the reply-to address and email layout, invitations, roles, and removing
      people. Only admins can delete the company.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Members</strong> work with jobs and applications. They can create, edit, publish, and delete jobs, set up a
      job's stages and stage emails, and move, comment on, export, and delete applications. They can also rank a job's
      applications again, which uses Credits.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Admins can change anyone's role and remove anyone, including other admins. Every company must always have at least
    one admin.
  </p>

  <h3>Your responsibility for your team</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      You're responsible for everything your team members do in Emploist, and for making sure they follow these Terms.
    </li>
    <li>Only invite people who need access to help you hire, and choose your admins carefully.</li>
    <li>When someone leaves your organization or no longer needs access, remove them from your company promptly.</li>
    <li>
      If you think someone has accessed your company without permission, tell us right away at <a
        href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a
      >.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="service">3. The service</h2>

  <h3>What Emploist includes</h3>
  <p>With your Plan, you can:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      publish jobs on a careers page at yourcompany.emploist.com, or on a subdomain of a domain you own, such as
      careers.yourcompany.com
    </li>
    <li>collect applications through forms you design</li>
    <li>rank applications with AI (see <a href="#ai-ranking">section 4</a>)</li>
    <li>move applicants through hiring stages and email them along the way</li>
    <li>comment on applications and mention your teammates</li>
    <li>export applications</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Emploist is a software tool. We aren't an employment agency or a recruiter, and we don't make hiring decisions for
    you.
  </p>

  <h3>Changes to the service</h3>
  <p>
    We're always improving Emploist, so we may change, add, or remove features. If a change would materially reduce
    what your Plan includes, we'll email your admins at least 30 days before it takes effect, and you can cancel before
    then. If we need to make such a change sooner for security or legal reasons, we'll tell you as soon as we reasonably
    can.
  </p>

  <h3>Fair use and technical limits</h3>
  <p>
    The price doesn't depend on the size of your team: there are no per-seat fees. We don't limit the number of jobs you
    can post. Emploist does have reasonable technical limits that keep it fast and reliable for everyone, for example on
    the number of team members, file sizes, the length of text, and how often requests and applications can be made. We
    may change these limits. If your use goes far beyond normal use for a hiring team, or puts the service or other
    customers at risk, we may limit it, and we'll contact you where we can.
  </p>

  <h3>Support and availability</h3>
  <p>
    We provide support by email at <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a>. Dodo Payments
    handles questions about payments, invoices, and refunds (see sections 5 and 6). We aim to reply quickly, but we don't
    promise response times. We work to keep Emploist available and reliable, but we don't offer a service level
    agreement or guarantee uptime. Emploist may sometimes be unavailable, for example during maintenance or because of
    problems with our providers or the internet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai-ranking">4. AI ranking</h2>

  <h3>What it does</h3>
  <p>
    When AI ranking is on, Emploist scores each application against the job it's for. It reads the resume and the
    applicant's answers to your questions, and compares them with the job's title, department, employment type, and
    description. Skills, experience, seniority fit, and, when the job asks for any degrees, certifications, or licenses,
    qualifications are each scored, then combined into a match percentage. By default, a job's applications are listed
    with the best matches first, and your team can see how each part was scored.
  </p>

  <h3>What it doesn't do</h3>
  <p>
    AI ranking never rejects, advances, moves, or emails anyone by itself. Only your team members move applications
    between stages. Emails to applicants go out only when an application arrives or when someone on your team moves an
    application, and only if you've turned on the email for that stage.
  </p>

  <h3>Turning it on and off</h3>
  <p>
    AI ranking is on by default when a company is created. While your Plan is active, admins can turn it off, or back
    on, for the whole company in Settings. While it's off, applications aren't sent to our AI provider, match scores are
    hidden, and new applications aren't ranked. When an admin turns it back on, Emploist asks whether to rank the
    applications that arrived while it was off, which sends them to our AI provider and uses Credits, or to leave them
    unranked. If you don't want AI to help review applications, turn it off before you start taking them.
  </p>

  <h3>Scores can be wrong</h3>
  <p>
    Match scores come from an AI model. They can be inaccurate or incomplete, and they can reflect gaps or bias in the
    resume, the answers, or your job description. A score isn't a finding about whether someone is qualified, and it
    isn't legal advice. Some applications can't be scored at all, for example when a resume is a scanned image.
  </p>

  <h3>How you must use it</h3>
  <p>
    You decide whether and how to use AI ranking, and you're responsible for that decision and for every hiring decision
    you make. If you use it, you must:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      use match scores only as one input, alongside a real review of the application by people who have the skills and
      authority to make the decision and to disagree with the score
    </li>
    <li>
      never make a decision about an applicant based only on a score, and never treat a low score, or a missing score, as
      a reason not to review an application
    </li>
    <li>
      tell applicants that AI helps review applications whenever AI ranking is on. While it's on, the default careers
      page shows this notice on job and application pages: "We use AI to help review applications against this job's
      requirements." It doesn't show it while AI ranking is off, so before you rank applications that arrived then, make
      sure those applicants were told in another way. If you use your own careers page code, add a notice like it
      yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      give any other notices, and meet any other requirements, that the law places on you for using AI in hiring. If you
      rely on scores in a way that makes AI ranking an automated employment decision tool under a law such as New York
      City's Local Law 144 (for example, by giving scores more weight than your other criteria, or by using them to
      overrule your team's judgment), you must meet that law's requirements, including bias audits and advance notices to
      candidates. Other examples include notices in job postings where those are required, and the duties of deployers of
      high-risk AI systems under the EU AI Act, such as human oversight, informing workers' representatives where the law
      requires it, and informing the people affected.
    </li>
    <li>
      handle applicants' requests about AI, such as requests for an explanation, a human review, or a different process,
      where the law gives them those rights
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You're responsible for complying with the employment, anti-discrimination, data protection, and AI laws that apply to
    your hiring. This section and the guidance in the app are our instructions for using AI ranking. We don't monitor how
    you use it.
  </p>

  <h3>Uses that aren't allowed</h3>
  <p>Don't use AI ranking:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      to screen applicants on protected characteristics, such as race, ethnicity, national origin, religion, caste, sex,
      gender identity, sexual orientation, age, or disability, or on things that stand in for them, such as postal codes
      or photos. This includes writing job details meant to favor or exclude any such group.
    </li>
    <li>
      to monitor, evaluate, promote, discipline, or dismiss employees or other workers, or for anything other than
      reviewing applications for the job they were submitted to. Reviewing an application that an employee submits for a
      job is fine.
    </li>
    <li>to make decisions automatically, or in any process that acts on scores without a person's review</li>
    <li>in a way that misleads anyone about the role of AI, for example by presenting a score as a person's judgment</li>
    <li>in a way that breaks the law or violates applicants' privacy or other rights</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Our AI provider</h3>
  <p>
    To rank an application, we send the resume text, as it is, including any names and contact details in it, and the
    applicant's answers (other than phone numbers), together with the job's details, to our AI provider, which is named
    in our <a href="/privacy/#subprocessors">list of subprocessors</a>. We don't use Customer Data to train AI models.
    Our AI provider's terms say it won't use the data we send to train its AI models without our consent, and we don't
    give that consent. Its terms also let it keep and use that data in other ways, as described in <a
      href="#data-processing">section 13</a
    >. We may change our AI provider or the model we use. A new AI provider is a new subprocessor, so we'll give notice
    of it as described in section 13. Scores already given don't change unless you rank the applications again.
  </p>

  <h3>Where AI ranking is available</h3>
  <p>
    We may limit or turn off AI ranking in some countries or regions, or for some companies, where the law requires it
    or where a law would require steps we can't reasonably take. We'll tell affected admins in advance where we can.
  </p>

  <h2 id="plans-and-payments">5. Plan, free trial, and payments</h2>

  <h3>The Plan</h3>
  <p>
    Emploist has one Plan, which you can pay for monthly or yearly, in advance, plus applicable taxes. Monthly billing
    costs US${PLAN.priceUsd} a month, and yearly billing costs US${PLAN.yearlyPriceUsd} a year. Either way, the Plan includes
    everything Emploist offers, and Credits each billing period (see below). Your Plan renews automatically at the end of
    each billing period, for another period of the same length, until it's canceled.
  </p>

  <h3>Switching to yearly billing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      An admin can switch a monthly Plan to yearly billing from the Billing page, as long as the Plan is set to renew and
      its payments are up to date.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you switch during the free trial, nothing is charged then. The trial goes on, and when it ends, Dodo charges the
      first yearly payment.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you switch after the trial, your yearly billing period starts that day. Dodo charges the yearly price, less a
      credit for the unused part of your current month, and the year's Credits replace any Plan Credits left from the
      month.
    </li>
    <li>If the payment for the switch fails, your Plan stays as it was.</li>
    <li>A yearly Plan can't be switched to monthly billing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Dodo Payments, our merchant of record</h3>
  <p>
    Our order process is conducted by our online reseller Dodo Payments ("Dodo"). Dodo is the merchant of record for all
    our orders. This means you buy your Plan and credit packs from Dodo, which handles checkout, charges your payment
    method, issues invoices and receipts, collects sales tax and VAT, and handles payment questions and refunds. Dodo's <a
      href="https://dodopayments.com/legal/buyer-terms">Buyer Terms</a
    > also apply to your purchase, and these Terms govern your use of Emploist. Your payment details go to Dodo, and we never
    see your full card number.
  </p>

  <h3>Taxes</h3>
  <p>
    Prices are in US dollars and don't include taxes. Dodo adds sales tax, VAT, GST, or similar taxes where they apply,
    and shows them at checkout.
  </p>

  <h3>Free trial</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Your first Plan can start with a free trial of {PLAN.trialDays} days, which includes {PLAN.trialCredits} Credits.</li>
    <li>
      You choose monthly or yearly billing and add a payment card at checkout, and you aren't charged during the trial.
      When the trial ends, Dodo charges the first monthly or yearly payment, unless you've canceled before then (see <a
        href="#refunds-and-cancellation">section 6</a
      >).
    </li>
    <li>
      If the trial's Credits run out before it ends, an admin can end the trial early from Emploist. Dodo then charges
      the first payment straight away, your first billing period starts that day, and its Credits replace the trial's.
    </li>
    <li>If you cancel during the trial, Emploist keeps working until the trial ends, and then your Plan ends.</li>
    <li>
      Each company, and each person, can have only one free trial. Don't create new accounts or companies, or ask others
      to, to get another trial.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>AI credits</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your Plan includes Credits each billing period: {formatNumber(PLAN.credits)} a month with monthly billing, or {
        formatNumber(PLAN.yearlyCredits)
      } a year with yearly billing. They're added all at once, when the payment for that
      period succeeds.
    </li>
    <li>
      Plan Credits don't carry over. When a new period's Credits are added, any Credits left from the previous period or
      the trial expire.
    </li>
    <li>
      Ranking one application successfully uses one Credit. If ranking fails or a resume can't be read, no Credit is used.
    </li>
    <li>
      Ranking an application again uses another Credit, for example when you change a job's details and choose to rank
      its applications again.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you run out of Credits, applications still arrive. They wait until an admin chooses to rank them, which uses
      Credits, or to skip ranking them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Credits can only be used while your Plan is active. They have no cash value, can't be transferred or sold, and
      aren't refundable, except where the law requires.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Credit packs</h3>
  <p>Admins can buy extra Credits in packs, plus applicable taxes:</p>
  <ul>
    {
      CREDIT_PACKS.map((pack) => (
        <li>
          {formatNumber(pack.credits)} Credits for US${pack.priceUsd}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Pack Credits never expire, but they can only be bought and used while your Plan is active. If your Plan ends,
      unused pack Credits are kept, and you can use them again once your Plan is active again.
    </li>
    <li>Your Plan's Credits are used before your pack Credits.</li>
    <li>
      Unused pack Credits are lost if your company is deleted, including if we delete it after 12 months without an
      active Plan (see section 14).
    </li>
    <li>
      If a pack is refunded, in full or in part, its remaining Credits are removed. Credits already used aren't affected.
    </li>
    <li>Pack prices may change, but changes don't affect packs you've already bought.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Failed payments</h3>
  <p>
    If a payment fails, Emploist keeps working for a short grace period, and the Billing page shows when it ends. If the
    payment still hasn't gone through by then, your company becomes read-only (see <a href="#refunds-and-cancellation"
      >section 6</a
    >) until an admin updates the payment method in Dodo's customer portal. Dodo may also cancel your Plan if it can't
    collect payment.
  </p>

  <h3>Chargebacks</h3>
  <p>
    Chargebacks and other billing disputes are handled by Dodo under its Buyer Terms, which ask you to contact Dodo
    before you raise a dispute with your bank or card issuer. While a charge is disputed, we may suspend your company.
  </p>

  <h3>Price changes</h3>
  <p>
    The Plan's price may change. We'll email your admins at least 30 days before a price increase applies to your
    existing Plan. The new price applies from your first billing period that starts after those 30 days. If you don't
    want to pay it, cancel before then. Dodo may also tell you about the change and, where the law requires, ask for your
    consent.
  </p>

  <h2 id="refunds-and-cancellation">6. Refunds and cancellation</h2>

  <h3>Canceling your Plan</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      An admin can cancel anytime from the Billing page in Emploist, which opens Dodo's customer portal. If that doesn't
      work for you, email us at <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}>{SITE.supportEmail}</a> and we'll help.
    </li>
    <li>
      Cancel before your free trial or billing period ends, and you won't be charged again. Your Plan keeps working until
      then, and an admin can undo the cancellation before it ends.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Refunds</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Payments are non-refundable, except where the law requires a refund, or if we stop offering Emploist (see <a
        href="#suspension-and-termination">section 14</a
      >). Otherwise, there are no refunds or credits for partial billing periods, unused time, unused Credits, or credit
      packs. The only exception is the credit for the unused part of your month when you switch to yearly billing (see
      <a href="#plans-and-payments">section 5</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      If you believe the law entitles you to a refund, email us at <a href={`mailto:${SITE.supportEmail}`}
        >{SITE.supportEmail}</a
      >. Refunds are only ever paid by Dodo, to the original payment method. Dodo's Buyer Terms say that refunds are at
      Dodo's discretion and that there are no refunds for unused subscription periods.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you're registered for sales tax, VAT, or GST and were charged it, you may be able to claim it back from Dodo. Dodo's
      Buyer Terms require you to contact Dodo within 60 days of the purchase.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Deleting your company</h3>
  <p>
    An admin can delete your company at any time in Settings. If you're the only person in your company and you delete
    your own account, your company is deleted too, in the same way. Deleting it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>cancels your Plan immediately, with no refund for the rest of the billing period or the trial</li>
    <li>
      permanently deletes your company's data, including its jobs, applications, resumes, and unused pack Credits, as
      described in section 13
    </li>
    <li>can't be undone, so export anything you need first</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>After your Plan ends</h3>
  <p>
    When your Plan ends, or when the grace period after a failed payment ends without payment, your company becomes
